#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Remove a Builder install: the inverse of `bin/builder-install`'s server
role for the single-account shape, and of `bin/provision-accounts` plus
`bin/install-privileged` for the two-account and three-account shapes.

    bin/uninstall-builder              stop and remove what the install placed
    bin/uninstall-builder --dry-run    say what would be removed, change nothing
    bin/uninstall-builder --keep-data  keep the Library's store and your backups,
                                       without being asked

The two-account shape (`agents` exists, `reviewers` does not, and
`install-privileged`'s own `install.json` at the prefix names shape "two")
and the three-account shape (`agents` and `reviewers` both exist, and that
same `install.json` names shape "three") both need root, since each stops
the agents account's own system-scope jobs, removes the root-owned tree
under the prefix, and removes the accounts themselves. The three-account
shape also removes its one sudo rule; the two-account shape has none to
remove, because `install-privileged --shape two` never places a sudoers
file, a review launcher or a `reviewers` account:

    sudo bin/uninstall-builder                run it
    sudo bin/uninstall-builder --dry-run       say what would be removed, change nothing
    sudo bin/uninstall-builder --purge-homes   also delete a home that still holds
                                               something, once --keep-data or the
                                               operator's answer has decided what that is

## WHAT THIS REMOVES, AND WHERE EACH LIST COMES FROM

This never keeps its own list of paths. Each is read off the installer that
placed it, the same way `bin/install-jobs --single-account` renders the
declarations in `jobs/` and `bin/link-commands --status` reports what it
linked:

- **Jobs.** `jobs/*.toml`, run through the same `as_invoker()` the install
  itself used, so the unit each job renders to is the one this stops and
  removes, but only once the supervisor is asked what it actually has loaded
  for that label or unit and that answer names this same file — the
  supervisor is a machine-wide singleton that `--home` does not scope, so a
  label match alone would stop and remove whatever real job happens to share
  it. Only then: `launchctl bootout` then the `.plist`, or `systemctl --user
  disable --now` then the `.service`/`.timer`, on whichever platform this
  runs on. Every string field of a resolved job — `tools/jobs/placeholders.py`'s
  own `_all_strings` — is read for a path under `{state}` or `{home}/.orient-control`,
  which is how `~/.local/state/orient`, `~/.local/state/library` and
  `~/.local/state/builder-backup` are found without a hand-kept list; the
  Library's store and the backups are held out of the automatic set and asked
  about separately, below.
- **Deploy areas.** `bin/install-backend`'s own `REPOSITORIES` and
  `COMPONENTS`, plus `bin/install-payload`'s own payload repository name,
  give the exact directories under `~/.local/deploy` (and, for the LLM
  gateway component, `~/.local/lib/llm-gateway` and
  `~/.local/venv-llm-gateway`, which `install-backend` places outside the
  deploy root because the gateway's own launcher hardcodes them). Each is
  removed only when it carries that installer's own stamp file — the same
  file `install-backend --status` reads to say what a deploy area is
  serving — so a directory this command did not make stays, the way
  `link-commands` already refuses to replace one it does not own.
- **Linked commands and skills.** `bin/link-commands`'s own `plan()`, read
  against the real `~/.local/bin` and `~/.claude/skills`, so only a link this
  command's own earlier run put there is removed; an occupied destination is
  left and named. The skills `install-payload` links directly —
  `new-crew`, `new-subcrew`, `take-upstream`, `write-bead` — are found the
  same way `install-payload` itself checks them: by what is in the
  `builder-payload` deploy area's own `skills/` directory.
- **The session hook.** The one entry `link-commands` added to
  `hooks.SessionStart` in `~/.claude/settings.json`, matched by its exact
  command. Everything else in that file is untouched.
- **The PATH line.** The one line `link-commands` prepended to the login
  shell's own non-login startup file (`~/.bashrc` for bash, `~/.zshenv` for
  zsh), matched by its exact text; the file it created is kept even once
  this leaves it empty, and everything else in it is untouched. A login
  shell `link-commands` wrote nothing for is skipped, the same way.
- **MCP registrations.** `bin/register-mcp`'s own `plan()`: a registration
  that still says what this install put there is removed with
  `claude mcp remove`; one that was changed to point somewhere else is left,
  for the same reason `register-mcp` itself will not touch a registration it
  did not write.
- **The knowledge index.** `~/.knowledge-index`
  — the derived index and its own logs (`index.db`, `index.db-shm`,
  `index.db-wal`, `autopull.log`, `watcher.log`) that agent-platform's
  `bin/knowledge-autopull`, `bin/agentctl` and `bin/co` all hardcode at
  `Path.home()/.knowledge-index`. No job declaration in `jobs/` names it, so
  it is removed by content rather than read off an installer: only when every
  entry in it is one of those five names. Anything else there is left and
  named.
- **The machine-wide instruction file** (single-account shape, where
  `install-payload` runs). `~/.claude/CLAUDE.md`, placed by
  `bin/install-payload` from this same release's `builder-payload` deploy
  area (`knowledge-seed/machine/CLAUDE.md`, its own `MACHINE_SOURCE`).
  Removed only when it is byte-identical to that shipped copy, compared
  before the deploy area goes; a file that differs —
  an edit, or a copy from a different release — or a missing shipped copy to
  compare against, is left and named, never guessed at.

## WHAT IT NEVER TOUCHES

The Builder root (`~/builder`: crews, the knowledge repository, the
steward's charter) holds the adopter's own work and is never named here. The
operator config, the `CREWS_ROOT` pin, and any secret the operator supplied
by hand (the LLM gateway's credentials file) are the operator's, not this
install's, and stay. A file or link this did not make stays, named in the
closing report. The knowledge index, and on the single-account shape the
machine-wide instruction file, are removed only when each matches what this
install itself would have made; an edited machine-wide file, or an index
holding anything beyond its own five known names, is left and named the
same way.

## THE TWO-ACCOUNT SHAPE: THE SAME REMOVALS, UNDER THE AGENTS HOME, PLUS THE
## ROOT-OWNED HALF AND THE ACCOUNT ITSELF

Detected by `agents` existing, `reviewers` not existing, and
`install-privileged`'s own `install.json` at the prefix (`/opt/builder` by
default) naming shape "two". Runs only as root, since every one of its steps
needs it:

- **Jobs.** The same `account = "agents"` declarations in `jobs/*.toml` the
  three-account shape removes, installed as LaunchDaemons or systemd system
  units by that same shape's own code, over the same system domain, under
  the same rule: a job is stopped only once the supervisor confirms what it
  has loaded for it IS the file this run is about to remove. Their logs live
  under the root-owned `--log-dir` (`{prefix}/var/log` by default), same as
  on the three-account shape.
- **Everything the single-account shape removes, pointed at the agents
  home.** The deploy areas, the linked commands and skills, the session
  hook, the MCP registrations, the state directories the declarations
  name, and the knowledge index the `agents` account's own
  `knowledge-autopull` job writes there — the exact same functions the
  three-account shape runs for this, with `--home` the agents account's home.
- **The root-owned tree under the prefix.** Removed whole, the same way the
  three-account shape removes it: its own `install.json` is this shape's
  detection marker too, so nothing else is expected to live there. This
  shape has no sudo rule, no review launcher and no `reviewers` account to
  remove — `install-privileged --shape two` never places any of the three —
  so this never looks for a sudoers file or a launcher path, and their
  absence is never reported as something left behind.
- **The account.** `bin/provision-accounts --shape two --remove`, last, so
  it is not gone out from under a step that still needed it. `--purge-homes`
  works the same way it does for the three-account shape.

GitHub's own state (the App installation, the branch rules on `main`) is out
of scope here too, for the same reason it is on the three-account shape.

## THE THREE-ACCOUNT SHAPE: THE SAME REMOVALS, UNDER THE AGENTS HOME, PLUS
## THE ROOT-OWNED HALF AND THE ACCOUNTS THEMSELVES

Detected by `agents` and `reviewers` both existing and `install-privileged`'s
own `install.json` sitting at the prefix (`/opt/builder` by default) naming
shape "three". Runs only as root, since every one of its steps needs it:

- **Jobs.** The agents account's own system-scope jobs — `account = "agents"`
  in `jobs/*.toml` — installed as LaunchDaemons or systemd system units,
  root's own and machine-wide, with `UserName=`/`User=` set to `agents`
  rather than as that account's per-user jobs. The same rule `stop_job`
  applies for the single-account shape governs here too, asked of the system
  domain these jobs actually load into (`system/<label>` on launchd, the
  system manager on systemd, never `--user`): stop a job only once the
  supervisor confirms what it has loaded for it IS the file this run is
  about to remove, and leave a mismatch running, named. Their logs live under
  the root-owned `--log-dir` (`{prefix}/var/log` by default), not the agents
  home, the same way `install-jobs` itself places them there for this shape.
- **Everything the single-account shape removes, pointed at the agents
  home.** The deploy areas, the linked commands and skills, the session
  hook, the MCP registrations, the state directories the declarations
  name, and the knowledge index the `agents` account's own
  `knowledge-autopull` job writes there — the exact same functions, run with
  `--home` the agents account's home instead of the operator's, because the
  three-account shape's `place`, `link` and `register` steps are `agents`'
  own, the same installers the single-account shape runs for the operator.
- **The root-owned tree under the prefix, and its one sudo rule.** The
  sudoers file is identified by its exact path (`{sudoers-dir}/builder-review`)
  AND its exact expected content — `install-privileged`'s own template for
  this prefix, rendered the same way `stage_sudo_rule` renders it — never by
  a pattern, so another file in that directory is never touched. A file at
  that path with different content is left and named, the same way an
  unstamped deploy area is. The prefix itself is then removed whole: its own
  `install.json` is this shape's detection marker, so nothing else is
  expected to live there.
- **The accounts.** `bin/provision-accounts --shape three --remove`, last, so
  the accounts are not gone out from under a step that still needed them.
  `--purge-homes` is this command's own flag for the same one
  `provision-accounts --shape three --remove` takes: without it, a home that
  still holds something — the Library's store or your backups, if `--keep-data`
  was given or you declined to remove them — is left, and `provision-accounts`
  says so rather than guessing.

GitHub's own state (the App installation, the branch rules on `main`) is out
of scope. Removing this machine's install never reaches GitHub, and the
closing report says so.

## AN UNFINISHED INSTALL, OR ONE WHOSE RECORD CONTRADICTS ITS ACCOUNTS, IS
## STILL OUT OF SCOPE

A shape's name counts every account on the machine, the operator's own
included: the two-account install is `agents` alone, with no `reviewers`
account at all, and `install-privileged`'s own `install.json` naming shape
"two"; the three-account install is `agents` and `reviewers` both, with that
same file naming shape "three". `agents` existing with no readable
`install.json` — the privileged install has not run yet — is an unfinished
install of either shape. `install.json` naming a shape the accounts present
contradict — "two" with `reviewers` also present, or "three" with
`reviewers` absent — is refused the same way: an installer never leaves the
machine in that state on its own, so it means something else changed the
accounts or the file by hand. Neither case is guessed at: both refuse, name
what was found, and leave the decision to the operator.
"""

from __future__ import annotations

import argparse
import importlib.machinery
import importlib.util
import json
import os
import shutil
import subprocess
import sys
import tempfile
import traceback
from collections import namedtuple
from pathlib import Path

# What `bin/install-jobs`'s own `drop_to_account` needs to become an account:
# its name, uid and primary gid. Built from `provision-accounts`' own
# `Account` (name, uid, gid, home) rather than a real `pwd.struct_passwd`, so
# a `FakeAccountSystem` answers this the same way it already answers
# `system.account()` everywhere else in this file.
AccountIds = namedtuple("AccountIds", "pw_name pw_uid pw_gid")

PROG = "uninstall-builder"
REPO = Path(__file__).resolve().parent.parent
sys.path.insert(0, str(REPO / "tools"))

from jobs import declaration, placeholders, render_launchd, render_systemd  # noqa: E402


class Refused(Exception):
    """Something the operator has to decide about. Never a stack trace."""


def _load(name: str, path: Path):
    """Load `path` as a module named `name`, registered in `sys.modules`
    before it runs.

    **REGISTERED BEFORE `exec_module`, WHICH IS WHAT `import` ITSELF DOES.**
    `install-backend` has `from __future__ import annotations`, so its
    `@dataclass` resolves annotations through `sys.modules[cls.__module__]`;
    absent from `sys.modules`, that lookup is `None` and the module fails to
    load with `AttributeError: 'NoneType' object has no attribute '__dict__'`
    — a failure in this loader that reads as a broken installer.
    """
    spec = importlib.util.spec_from_loader(name, importlib.machinery.SourceFileLoader(name, str(path)))
    module = importlib.util.module_from_spec(spec)
    sys.modules[name] = module
    spec.loader.exec_module(module)
    return module


# Each sibling installer, loaded as a module rather than re-typed, so what
# this removes can never drift from what that one places. None of these
# scripts does anything at import time beyond defining functions and
# constants; the `if __name__ == "__main__":` guard at the foot of each
# means importing it runs none of its own command-line behaviour.
#
# **`install-privileged` IS NEVER LOADED THIS WAY.** Unlike every module
# here, it calls its own `running_copy()` at import time, outside any
# `__main__` guard, and that function's whole job is to `sys.exit(2)` unless
# the file sits at `<prefix>/src/shipwright@<sha>/bin/install-privileged`.
# Importing it from this checkout would hit exactly that exit. What this
# needs from it — the sudoers rule's name and template, the prefix, the
# launcher's path once installed — are constants below instead, matching its
# own hardcoded values rather than re-deriving them from a module this can
# never safely load.
ij = _load("builder_uninstall_install_jobs", REPO / "bin" / "install-jobs")
lc = _load("builder_uninstall_link_commands", REPO / "bin" / "link-commands")
rm = _load("builder_uninstall_register_mcp", REPO / "bin" / "register-mcp")
ib = _load("builder_uninstall_install_backend", REPO / "bin" / "install-backend")
su = _load("builder_uninstall_stand_up_backend", REPO / "bin" / "stand-up-backend")
pa = _load("builder_uninstall_provision_accounts", REPO / "bin" / "provision-accounts")

AGENTS = su.AGENTS
REVIEWERS = next(name for name in pa.SERVICE_ACCOUNTS if name != AGENTS)
PAYLOAD_REPOSITORY = lc.PAYLOAD_REPOSITORY  # "builder-payload"
PAYLOAD_STAMP = ".installed"  # bin/install-payload's own STAMP, beside the deploy dir

# install-privileged's own constants, duplicated rather than imported (see
# above): the prefix it installs to, the sudoers file's directory and name,
# the launcher's path once live, and its sudoers template's path in this
# checkout.
DEFAULT_PREFIX = "/opt/builder"
DEFAULT_SUDOERS_DIR = "/etc/sudoers.d"
SUDOERS_NAME = "builder-review"
LAUNCHER_REL = os.path.join("libexec", "review-launch")
SUDOERS_TEMPLATE_REL = os.path.join("privileged", "builder-review.sudoers")
PRIVILEGED_INSTALL_JSON = "install.json"


def detect_platform() -> str:
    if sys.platform == "darwin":
        return "macos"
    if sys.platform.startswith("linux"):
        return "linux"
    raise Refused(
        f"{sys.platform} is neither macos nor linux, and Builder installs on "
        f"only those two. Nothing was removed."
    )


def read_install_shape(install_json: str) -> str | None:
    """The `shape` field `install-privileged` wrote to `install_json`, or
    `None` when the file is absent, unreadable, not JSON, not an object, or
    names no `shape` at all.

    **NEVER RAISES.** The caller treats every one of those cases as "no
    readable shape", lumped in with an install that has not finished writing
    the file yet — the same thing a stranger reading this later would want
    to know: there is nothing here to trust.
    """
    try:
        text = Path(install_json).read_text(encoding="utf-8")
    except OSError:
        return None
    try:
        data = json.loads(text)
    except ValueError:
        return None
    if not isinstance(data, dict):
        return None
    shape = data.get("shape")
    return shape if isinstance(shape, str) else None


def refuse_unsupported_shape(exists=None, prefix: str | None = None) -> str:
    """Returns "single" on a machine none of the multi-account installs ever
    touched, "two" where the two-account install has fully landed, and
    "three" where the three-account install has fully landed. Refuses
    outright on anything between.

    **NAMED BY THE ACCOUNTS AND THE PRIVILEGED INSTALL'S OWN MARKER, NOT BY
    ASKING WHAT ELSE IS INSTALLED.** A shape's name counts every account on
    the machine, the operator's own included: the two-account install is
    `agents` alone, with no `reviewers` account at all, and
    `install-privileged`'s own `install.json` at the prefix naming shape
    "two"; the three-account install is `agents` and `reviewers` both, with
    that same file naming shape "three". `agents` exists only on a machine
    `sudo bin/provision-accounts` ran on.

    `agents` existing with `install.json` naming no readable shape — the
    file is missing, unreadable, not JSON, or names none — is an unfinished
    install of either shape: the privileged install has not run yet. A
    readable shape that the accounts present contradict — "two" named while
    `reviewers` also exists, or "three" named while `reviewers` is absent —
    is refused the same way, since an installer never leaves a machine in
    that state on its own. Neither case is guessed at: both refuse, name
    what was found, and leave the decision to the operator.
    """
    exists = exists or su._account_exists
    prefix = DEFAULT_PREFIX if prefix is None else prefix
    if not exists(AGENTS):
        return "single"
    has_reviewers = exists(REVIEWERS)
    install_json = os.path.join(prefix, PRIVILEGED_INSTALL_JSON)
    shape = read_install_shape(install_json)
    if shape == "two" and not has_reviewers:
        return "two"
    if shape == "three" and has_reviewers:
        return "three"
    if shape is None:
        state = (f"`{REVIEWERS}` also exists" if has_reviewers
                 else f"no `{REVIEWERS}` account exists")
        raise Refused(
            f"the `{AGENTS}` account exists and {state}, but {install_json} does "
            f"not name a readable shape (it is missing, unreadable, not JSON, or "
            f"names none). This command supports only the two-account install "
            f"(`{AGENTS}` alone, with {install_json} naming shape \"two\") and the "
            f"three-account install (`{AGENTS}` and `{REVIEWERS}` both, with that "
            f"same file naming shape \"three\"); today this machine is an "
            f"unfinished install of one or the other — the privileged install has "
            f"not run yet. Nothing was removed."
        )
    raise Refused(
        f"{install_json} names shape {shape!r}, but `{REVIEWERS}` "
        f"{'exists' if has_reviewers else 'does not exist'}, which contradicts it. "
        f"This command supports only the two-account install (`{AGENTS}` alone, "
        f"shape \"two\") and the three-account install (`{AGENTS}` and "
        f"`{REVIEWERS}` both, shape \"three\"); today this machine matches "
        f"neither. Nothing was removed."
    )


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Jobs: stop and remove what `install-jobs --single-account` installed.
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------


def declarations_present(jobs_dir: Path) -> bool:
    return jobs_dir.is_dir() and any(jobs_dir.glob("*.toml"))


def raw_jobs(jobs_dir: Path, platform: str) -> list[declaration.Job]:
    """Every declared job, filtered to this platform and mapped to user-scope,
    but with no placeholder resolved yet.

    The same declarations `resolved_jobs` resolves, kept as templates so
    `state_plan` can tell which of a resolved job's fields are safe to derive
    a removal target from: a field's *template*, not its resolved text, is
    what says whether `{root}` — the one placeholder read from the
    agent-writable `~/.config/convivy/session-agent.env` — fed it.
    """
    declared = ij.load_jobs(jobs_dir, platform)
    return ij.as_invoker(declared)


def resolved_jobs(jobs_dir: Path, platform: str, home: str, root: str,
                   deploy: str, state: str, bin_dir: str) -> list[declaration.Job]:
    """Every declared job, as the single-account install rendered it.

    Mirrors `bin/install-jobs --single-account`: loaded, filtered to this
    platform, and run through the same `as_invoker()` that turns each
    system-scope job naming an account into a user-scope job of whoever runs
    it. Placeholders are resolved against this machine's layout so the
    fields that name a real path — directories, logs, environment values —
    are the literal paths the install wrote, not the templates.
    """
    declared = raw_jobs(jobs_dir, platform)
    machine = placeholders.Machine.for_home(
        home, root=root, python=sys.executable, deploy=deploy, state=state, bin=bin_dir,
    )
    # The value of a program's path is never read for removal — only whether
    # a declared `{name}` resolves at all — so a placeholder stands in for
    # it rather than a real `shutil.which` lookup.
    programs = {name: "" for job in declared for name in job.programs}
    out = []
    for job in declared:
        try:
            out.append(placeholders.resolve(job, machine, programs))
        except placeholders.PlaceholderError as exc:
            raise Refused(f"{exc} Nothing was removed.") from exc
    return out


def job_unit_paths(job: declaration.Job, platform: str, home: str) -> list[str]:
    """Every file `install-jobs` would have written for this (already
    user-scope) job, from `install-jobs`'s own `destinations()`."""
    return sorted(ij.destinations(job, platform, home).keys())


def loaded_launchd_path(label: str, run=subprocess.run) -> str | None:
    """The plist path launchd currently has loaded for `label` in this
    account's GUI domain, or `None` when nothing is loaded for it.

    `launchctl print gui/<uid>/<label>` exits 113 (`stand-up-backend`'s own
    `LAUNCHCTL_ABSENT`) when the domain has nothing loaded for `label`, read
    here the same as any other non-zero exit or a reply with no `path =`
    line: all three mean there is nothing loaded to compare against, so the
    caller leaves the job alone rather than guessing.
    """
    result = run(["launchctl", "print", f"gui/{os.getuid()}/{label}"],
                 capture_output=True, text=True, check=False)
    if result.returncode != 0:
        return None
    for line in (result.stdout or "").splitlines():
        stripped = line.strip()
        if stripped.startswith("path = "):
            return stripped[len("path = "):].strip()
    return None


def loaded_systemd_fragment(unit: str, run=subprocess.run) -> str | None:
    """The unit file path systemd currently has loaded for `unit`, in this
    account's user manager, or `None` when nothing is loaded for it.

    `systemctl --user show -p FragmentPath` answers a unit systemd has never
    heard of with exit 0 and an empty `FragmentPath=`, read here the same as
    a non-zero exit: both mean nothing is loaded to compare against.
    """
    result = run(["systemctl", "--user", "show", "-p", "FragmentPath", unit],
                 capture_output=True, text=True, check=False)
    if result.returncode != 0:
        return None
    for line in (result.stdout or "").splitlines():
        if line.startswith("FragmentPath="):
            value = line[len("FragmentPath="):].strip()
            return value or None
    return None


def stop_job(job: declaration.Job, platform: str, home: str,
             left: list[tuple[str, str]], run=subprocess.run) -> bool:
    """Ask the supervisor to stop this job, but only once it is confirmed
    that what the supervisor has loaded for it IS the file this run is about
    to remove.

    **THE SUPERVISOR IS A MACHINE-WIDE SINGLETON; `--home` SCOPES ONLY THE
    FILES THIS DELETES.** `launchctl bootout gui/<uid>/<label>` and
    `systemctl --user disable --now <unit>` both act against the real,
    running account's service manager, by label or unit name alone, whatever
    `--home` says — a security reviewer running this against a throwaway
    `--home` had it boot out a dozen of the operator's own real launchd jobs,
    because every job here shares its label with whatever real job happens to
    be running under that name. So before touching anything, this reads what
    the supervisor currently has loaded for the label (macOS) or each unit
    (Linux) and compares it, resolved with symlinks followed, against the
    plist or unit file this run is about to remove. A mismatch is left alone
    and named, with the path that IS loaded, in `left`. A label or unit
    nothing is loaded for is left alone without comment, the same as a
    supervisor that is not running at all: both fail the way a job that WAS
    running and is now stopped does not, and removing the files is what
    actually matters there.

    Returns whether a stop was actually issued (`launchctl bootout` or
    `systemctl --user disable --now`). **A CALLER MUST READ THIS BEFORE
    ACTING ON THE SUPERVISOR AGAIN** — `remove_jobs`'s `systemctl --user
    daemon-reload` is scoped to this, not to whether the job merely resolved
    to a unit path, since a mismatch or an absent load means nothing here was
    touched for this run to reload.
    """
    if platform == "macos":
        if not shutil.which("launchctl"):
            return False
        unit_paths = job_unit_paths(job, platform, home)
        loaded = loaded_launchd_path(job.label, run)
        if loaded is None:
            return False
        expected = unit_paths[0] if unit_paths else None
        if expected is None or os.path.realpath(loaded) != os.path.realpath(expected):
            left.append((job.label, f"launchd has {loaded} loaded for this label, not the "
                                    f"file this uninstall is removing; left running"))
            return False
        target = f"gui/{os.getuid()}/{job.label}"
        run(["launchctl", "bootout", target], capture_output=True, check=False)
        return True
    if not shutil.which("systemctl"):
        return False
    stopped = False
    for path in job_unit_paths(job, platform, home):
        unit = Path(path).name
        fragment = loaded_systemd_fragment(unit, run)
        if fragment is None:
            continue
        if os.path.realpath(fragment) != os.path.realpath(path):
            left.append((path, f"systemd has {fragment} loaded for {unit}, not the file "
                               f"this uninstall is removing; left running"))
            continue
        run(["systemctl", "--user", "disable", "--now", unit],
            capture_output=True, check=False)
        stopped = True
    return stopped


def remove_jobs(jobs: list[declaration.Job], platform: str, home: str, machine_state: str,
                 dry_run: bool, left: list[tuple[str, str]], removed: list[str],
                 run=subprocess.run) -> None:
    reloaded_linux = False
    for job in jobs:
        unit_paths = job_unit_paths(job, platform, home)
        log_paths = sorted({p for p in (job.stdout, job.stderr) if p})
        if dry_run:
            for path in unit_paths:
                if os.path.lexists(path):
                    print(f"  would stop and remove job unit: {job.label} ({path})")
            for path in log_paths:
                if os.path.lexists(path):
                    print(f"  would remove log: {path}")
            continue
        stopped = stop_job(job, platform, home, left, run)
        for path in unit_paths:
            if remove_path(Path(path), left):
                removed.append(path)
        for path in log_paths:
            if remove_path(Path(path), left):
                removed.append(path)
        if platform == "linux" and stopped:
            reloaded_linux = True
    if reloaded_linux and shutil.which("systemctl"):
        run(["systemctl", "--user", "daemon-reload"], capture_output=True, check=False)
    record_dir = ij.record_path(
        placeholders.Machine.for_home(home, root="", python=sys.executable, state=machine_state)
    ).parent
    if dry_run:
        if os.path.lexists(record_dir):
            print(f"  would remove: {record_dir}")
    else:
        if remove_path(record_dir, left):
            removed.append(str(record_dir))


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Jobs, three-account shape: stop and remove what the privileged half's own
# `install-jobs` run rendered for the agents account.
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------


def agents_system_jobs(jobs_dir: Path, platform: str) -> list[declaration.Job]:
    """Every declared job the three-account shape's root half installs: kept
    at system scope, naming `agents`, the same filter `leave_out_as_root`
    leaves standing — every job that is not a user-scope job with no
    account. Kept as templates, unresolved, the same reason `raw_jobs` keeps
    the single-account ones: so `state_plan` can tell a field `{state}` alone
    fed from one `{root}` reached."""
    declared = ij.load_jobs(jobs_dir, platform)
    return [job for job in declared if job.scope == "system" and job.account == AGENTS]


def resolved_agents_jobs(jobs_dir: Path, platform: str, agents_home: str, root: str,
                          log_dir: str, deploy: str, state: str, bin_dir: str) -> list[declaration.Job]:
    """Every agents-account job, resolved the way `install-jobs`' own
    `resolve()` resolves it for this shape: against the agents account's own
    home — already known here, so no directory-service lookup is needed —
    with its logs moved under the root-owned `log_dir`, since a system-scope
    job that runs as an account never logs into that account's home (root
    would open a path the account could re-point).

    **`deploy`, `state` AND `bin_dir` ARE THE CALLER'S OWN, NEVER LEFT TO
    `Machine.for_home`'s DEFAULTS.** Without them, a `--deploy`, `--state`
    or `--bin` the operator actually gave resolves every job field against
    the agents-home default instead — `state_plan`'s own removal set, and
    `remove_system_jobs`'s own record directory, would then be derived from
    a layout this run was never told to use, finding nothing to remove at
    the layout that is actually there. `resolved_jobs`, the single-account
    equivalent, threads the same three for the same reason.
    """
    declared = agents_system_jobs(jobs_dir, platform)
    machine = placeholders.Machine.for_home(
        agents_home, root=root, python=sys.executable, deploy=deploy, state=state, bin=bin_dir,
    )
    # The value of a program's path is never read for removal — only whether
    # a declared `{name}` resolves at all — so a placeholder stands in for
    # it rather than a real `shutil.which` lookup (see `resolved_jobs`).
    programs = {name: "" for job in declared for name in job.programs}
    out = []
    for job in declared:
        try:
            done = placeholders.resolve(job, machine, programs)
        except placeholders.PlaceholderError as exc:
            raise Refused(f"{exc} Nothing was removed.") from exc
        if done.stdout or done.stderr:
            done = ij.account_log_paths(done, log_dir)
        out.append(done)
    return out


def system_job_unit_paths(job: declaration.Job, platform: str, system_dir: str | None) -> list[str]:
    """Every file `install-jobs` would have written for this system-scope
    job, by path alone.

    **NEVER THROUGH `ij.destinations()`, WHICH RENDERS THE FILE'S CONTENT
    TOO.** A job that names an account renders `UserName=`/`User=` by asking
    the real directory service for that account (`render_launchd.render`,
    `render_systemd.render`, both through `accounts.require`), which this
    command has no business doing just to find a path to remove, and which
    fails outright wherever that lookup cannot run — a test's throwaway
    machine, or a real one between `provision-accounts` creating `agents`
    and the directory service settling. The path itself needs no such
    lookup on either platform (`render_launchd.install_path`,
    `render_systemd.unit_directory` and `needs_timer`), so this computes it
    directly rather than paying for content it never reads.
    """
    directory = system_dir or (
        "/Library/LaunchDaemons" if platform == "macos" else "/etc/systemd/system"
    )
    if platform == "macos":
        return [f"{directory}/{job.label}.plist"]
    names = [f"{job.label}.service"]
    if render_systemd.needs_timer(job):
        names.append(f"{job.label}.timer")
    return sorted(f"{directory}/{name}" for name in names)


def loaded_launchd_system_path(label: str, run=subprocess.run) -> str | None:
    """The plist path launchd currently has loaded for `label` in the SYSTEM
    domain, or `None` when nothing is loaded for it. See `loaded_launchd_path`
    for why a non-zero exit and no `path =` line both mean the same thing."""
    result = run(["launchctl", "print", f"system/{label}"],
                 capture_output=True, text=True, check=False)
    if result.returncode != 0:
        return None
    for line in (result.stdout or "").splitlines():
        stripped = line.strip()
        if stripped.startswith("path = "):
            return stripped[len("path = "):].strip()
    return None


def loaded_systemd_system_fragment(unit: str, run=subprocess.run) -> str | None:
    """The unit file path systemd's SYSTEM manager currently has loaded for
    `unit`, or `None`. See `loaded_systemd_fragment` for why a non-zero exit
    and an empty `FragmentPath=` both mean the same thing."""
    result = run(["systemctl", "show", "-p", "FragmentPath", unit],
                 capture_output=True, text=True, check=False)
    if result.returncode != 0:
        return None
    for line in (result.stdout or "").splitlines():
        if line.startswith("FragmentPath="):
            value = line[len("FragmentPath="):].strip()
            return value or None
    return None


def stop_system_job(job: declaration.Job, platform: str, system_dir: str | None,
                     left: list[tuple[str, str]], run=subprocess.run) -> bool:
    """`stop_job`'s own rule, asked of the domain these jobs actually load
    into. **NEVER THE AGENTS ACCOUNT'S OWN GUI OR `--user` DOMAIN.** A
    system-scope job that names an account is installed as a LaunchDaemon or
    a system-manager unit — root's own, machine-wide — with `UserName=`/
    `User=` set to the account it runs as, not as that account's own
    per-user job; a service account provisioned with no login session does
    not even have a `--user` manager running to ask. So this reads what the
    SYSTEM domain currently has loaded for the label (macOS) or each unit
    (Linux) and compares it, resolved with symlinks followed, against the
    file this run is about to remove — the same comparison `stop_job` makes,
    over the domain this shape's jobs are actually in. A mismatch is left
    running and named, with the path that IS loaded; a label or unit nothing
    is loaded for, or a supervisor not on PATH, is left alone without comment.

    Returns whether a stop was actually issued, for the same reason
    `stop_job`'s own return does: a caller's systemd reload is scoped to this.
    """
    if platform == "macos":
        if not shutil.which("launchctl"):
            return False
        unit_paths = system_job_unit_paths(job, platform, system_dir)
        loaded = loaded_launchd_system_path(job.label, run)
        if loaded is None:
            return False
        expected = unit_paths[0] if unit_paths else None
        if expected is None or os.path.realpath(loaded) != os.path.realpath(expected):
            left.append((job.label, f"launchd has {loaded} loaded for this label, not the "
                                    f"file this uninstall is removing; left running"))
            return False
        run(["launchctl", "bootout", f"system/{job.label}"], capture_output=True, check=False)
        return True
    if not shutil.which("systemctl"):
        return False
    stopped = False
    for path in system_job_unit_paths(job, platform, system_dir):
        unit = Path(path).name
        fragment = loaded_systemd_system_fragment(unit, run)
        if fragment is None:
            continue
        if os.path.realpath(fragment) != os.path.realpath(path):
            left.append((path, f"systemd has {fragment} loaded for {unit}, not the file "
                               f"this uninstall is removing; left running"))
            continue
        run(["systemctl", "disable", "--now", unit], capture_output=True, check=False)
        stopped = True
    return stopped


def remove_system_jobs(jobs: list[declaration.Job], platform: str, system_dir: str | None,
                        log_dir: str, agents_home: str, state: str, dry_run: bool,
                        left: list[tuple[str, str]], removed: list[str],
                        run=subprocess.run) -> None:
    """`remove_jobs`'s own shape, over the system domain and the root-owned
    `log_dir` the three-account shape actually uses. The `install-jobs`
    record this removes lives under `state` — the caller's own, never
    `agents_home`'s default — since a job naming an account resolves
    `{state}` against whatever `--state` the operator gave `install-jobs`,
    the same reason `remove_jobs`'s own `record_dir` takes `machine_state`
    rather than deriving it from `home` alone."""
    reloaded_linux = False
    for job in jobs:
        unit_paths = system_job_unit_paths(job, platform, system_dir)
        log_paths = sorted({p for p in (job.stdout, job.stderr) if p})
        if dry_run:
            for path in unit_paths:
                if os.path.lexists(path):
                    print(f"  would stop and remove job unit: {job.label} ({path})")
            for path in log_paths:
                if os.path.lexists(path):
                    print(f"  would remove log: {path}")
            continue
        stopped = stop_system_job(job, platform, system_dir, left, run)
        for path in unit_paths:
            if remove_path(Path(path), left):
                removed.append(path)
        for path in log_paths:
            if remove_path(Path(path), left):
                removed.append(path)
        if platform == "linux" and stopped:
            reloaded_linux = True
    if reloaded_linux and shutil.which("systemctl"):
        run(["systemctl", "daemon-reload"], capture_output=True, check=False)
    record_dir = ij.record_path(
        placeholders.Machine.for_home(agents_home, root="", python=sys.executable, state=state)
    ).parent
    if dry_run:
        if os.path.lexists(record_dir):
            print(f"  would remove: {record_dir}")
    else:
        if remove_path(record_dir, left):
            removed.append(str(record_dir))


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Running the agents account's own steps as that account, never as root.
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------


def run_as_agents_account(ids, action, drop=None) -> tuple[list[tuple[str, str]], list[str]]:
    """Run `action()` — which touches only the `agents` account's own home
    and returns `(left, removed)` — in a child process that has dropped to
    `ids` first, and return what the child reports.

    **A SEPARATE PROCESS, NOT A PERMANENT DROP.** `main_three_account` still
    needs root after this returns, to stop the system-scope jobs, remove
    the sudo rule and the prefix, and remove the accounts themselves — and
    a real `os.setuid` cannot be undone in the same process the way `bin/
    install-jobs`'s own `as_account` already forks for exactly this reason.
    `drop` is that file's own `drop_to_account` by default; a test that
    cannot become another account injects a no-op instead, the same way
    `tools/test_install_jobs.py` already does for `as_account`.

    **`(left, removed)` CROSS THE FORK OVER A PIPE**, the one thing that
    does not survive it on its own: a list `action()` mutates in the child
    is the child's own copy, invisible to this process once it returns.
    Anything `action()` raises from the refusal family every sibling
    installer uses is reported the same way a `Refused` raised directly in
    this process would be; anything else is a crash, printed as a
    traceback in the child (the same fd this process's own stdout and
    stderr already write to) before this raises in its place.
    """
    drop = ij.drop_to_account if drop is None else drop
    read_fd, write_fd = os.pipe()
    sys.stdout.flush()
    sys.stderr.flush()
    pid = os.fork()
    if pid == 0:
        os.close(read_fd)
        try:
            drop(ids)
            left, removed = action()
            payload = json.dumps({"left": left, "removed": removed}).encode("utf-8")
            status = 0
        except (Refused, ij.Refused, lc.Refused, rm.Refused, ib.Refused, pa.Refused) as exc:
            payload = json.dumps({"error": str(exc)}).encode("utf-8")
            status = 1
        except BaseException:
            traceback.print_exc()
            payload = b""
            status = 1
        sys.stdout.flush()
        sys.stderr.flush()
        try:
            with os.fdopen(write_fd, "wb") as out:
                out.write(len(payload).to_bytes(8, "big"))
                out.write(payload)
        finally:
            os._exit(status)
    os.close(write_fd)
    with os.fdopen(read_fd, "rb") as inp:
        header = inp.read(8)
        size = int.from_bytes(header, "big") if header else 0
        data = inp.read(size) if size else b""
    _, wait = os.waitpid(pid, 0)
    status = os.WEXITSTATUS(wait) if os.WIFEXITED(wait) else 1
    decoded = json.loads(data.decode("utf-8")) if data else {}
    if status != 0 or "error" in decoded:
        reason = decoded.get("error") or (
            "the child exited without reporting why; see its traceback above"
        )
        raise Refused(
            f"removing what is inside the `{AGENTS}` account's own home failed: "
            f"{reason}. Nothing further was removed."
        )
    left = [tuple(item) for item in decoded.get("left", [])]
    removed = list(decoded.get("removed", []))
    return left, removed


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The root-owned tree under the prefix, and its one sudo rule.
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------


def expected_sudoers_text(prefix: str) -> bytes:
    """The sudoers rule `install-privileged` would write for this prefix:
    its own template, read from this checkout, with its one placeholder
    filled in the way `stage_sudo_rule` fills it. Read from this checkout
    rather than kept as a copy, the same reason every removal above reads
    off today's sibling installer instead of its own list."""
    template = (REPO / SUDOERS_TEMPLATE_REL).read_text(encoding="utf-8")
    launcher = os.path.join(prefix, LAUNCHER_REL)
    return template.replace("@LAUNCHER@", launcher).encode("utf-8")


def remove_sudo_rule(prefix: str, sudoers_dir: str, dry_run: bool,
                      left: list[tuple[str, str]], removed: list[str]) -> None:
    """Remove the one sudo rule `install-privileged` wrote, identified by
    its exact path (`{sudoers_dir}/builder-review`) AND its exact expected
    content for this prefix — never by a pattern, so another file in
    `sudoers_dir` is never read, let alone touched. A file at that path
    whose content does not match — a different prefix, an edited rule, or
    something else entirely — is left and named, the same way an unstamped
    deploy area is."""
    path = Path(sudoers_dir) / SUDOERS_NAME
    if not os.path.lexists(path):
        return
    if path.is_symlink():
        left.append((str(path), "it is a symlink; refusing to remove through it"))
        return
    try:
        live = path.read_bytes()
    except OSError as exc:
        left.append((str(path), f"could not be read ({exc.strerror or exc})"))
        return
    if live != expected_sudoers_text(prefix):
        left.append((str(path), "exists but its content is not what install-privileged "
                                "would write for this prefix, so it is left"))
        return
    if dry_run:
        print(f"  would remove: {path}")
        return
    if remove_path(path, left):
        removed.append(str(path))


def remove_privileged_tree(prefix: str, dry_run: bool,
                           left: list[tuple[str, str]], removed: list[str]) -> None:
    """Remove the root-owned tree under `prefix` whole: the extracted
    source trees, the reviewers' tools and venv, the launcher, every
    credential slot, and `install.json` itself. Its presence is this
    shape's own detection marker, so nothing else is expected to live
    there — unlike a deploy area, which can sit beside one install never
    stamped, this prefix is never shared with anything else."""
    if not os.path.lexists(prefix):
        return
    if dry_run:
        print(f"  would remove: {prefix}")
        return
    if remove_path(Path(prefix), left):
        removed.append(prefix)


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Removal primitive, shared by every step below.
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------


def remove_path(path: Path, left: list[tuple[str, str]]) -> bool:
    """Remove a file or directory, never through a symlink, and never by
    crashing: a removal that fails is reported in the closing list rather
    than stopping the rest of the run, the way `install-jobs`'s own
    `save_record` treats a failure as something to report, not to raise.

    Returns whether `path` was actually removed. **A CALLER MUST READ THIS
    BEFORE COUNTING `path` AS REMOVED.** A symlink refusal or an `OSError`
    both leave `path` exactly where it was, having already added it to `left`
    with the reason; a caller that appends to its own `removed` list
    unconditionally, rather than on this return value, reports a failure as a
    success in the closing counts.
    """
    try:
        if not os.path.lexists(path):
            return False
        if os.path.islink(path):
            left.append((str(path), "it is a symlink; refusing to remove through it"))
            return False
        if path.is_dir():
            shutil.rmtree(path)
        else:
            os.remove(path)
        return True
    except OSError as exc:
        left.append((str(path), f"could not be removed ({exc.strerror or exc})"))
        return False


def unlink_path(path: Path, left: list[tuple[str, str]]) -> bool:
    """Remove exactly this symlink, never what it points at.

    **THE OPPOSITE CHECK FROM `remove_path`, ON PURPOSE.** A deploy area or a
    state directory is never supposed to be a symlink, so `remove_path`
    refuses one as a sign something is wrong. A linked command or skill is
    always a symlink — that is what `link-commands` and `install-payload`
    put there — so refusing one here would refuse to undo the one thing this
    step exists to undo. The caller has already checked, through `classify()`
    or an equivalent comparison, that this exact link still points where it
    is expected to; this only re-confirms it is still a symlink at all before
    acting, the way `install-jobs`'s own writes re-check state they already
    saw once.
    """
    try:
        if not path.is_symlink():
            left.append((str(path), "changed since this was planned; it is no longer a symlink"))
            return False
        os.unlink(path)
        return True
    except OSError as exc:
        left.append((str(path), f"could not be removed ({exc.strerror or exc})"))
        return False


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Builder state under ~/.local/state, and ~/.orient-control.
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------

LIBRARY_NAME = "library"
BACKUP_NAME = "builder-backup"
ORIENT_CONTROL_NAME = ".orient-control"


def _state_top_name(state_norm: str, text: str) -> str | None:
    """`text`'s first path segment below `state_norm`, or `None` when `text`
    does not collapse to a strict descendant of `state_norm` at all.

    **COLLAPSED (`os.path.normpath`) BEFORE THE FIRST SEGMENT IS EVER
    TAKEN**, never matched by plain substring against `state` the way an
    earlier version did. A `text.startswith(state + "/")` check reads
    `{state}/./knowledge` as living under `state` and hands back the literal
    segment `"."` — and an operator-writable `CREWS_ROOT`
    (`~/.config/convivy/session-agent.env`, which any agent session can
    write) of `{state}/.` turns `LIBRARY_KNOWLEDGE_ROOT = "{root}/knowledge"`
    into exactly that text, making `"."` the auto-removal set's one entry:
    the whole `state` directory, Library store and backups included,
    bypassing `--keep-data` and the consent prompt both. Collapsing first
    turns that same text into `{state}/knowledge`, whose segment is the
    ordinary name `"knowledge"`; a `CREWS_ROOT` of `{state}/..` collapses to
    `state`'s own parent, which no longer starts with `state_norm` at all
    once collapsed, so nothing is derived from it either. A resulting
    segment that is still empty, `"."`, `".."` or holds a separator — none of
    which a genuine child name ever is — is refused rather than trusted.
    """
    normalized = os.path.normpath(text)
    if normalized != state_norm and not normalized.startswith(state_norm + os.sep):
        return None
    rel = os.path.relpath(normalized, state_norm)
    top = rel.split(os.sep, 1)[0]
    if not top or top in (".", "..") or os.sep in top:
        return None
    return top


def state_plan(jobs: list[declaration.Job], templates: list[declaration.Job],
               home: str, state: str) -> tuple[set[str], bool]:
    """Top-level names under `state` the declarations reference, and whether
    `{home}/.orient-control` is one of them.

    **DERIVED FROM EVERY STRING FIELD OF EACH RESOLVED JOB**, through
    `placeholders._all_strings`, the same generator the installer's own
    portability lint reads — not from a hand-kept list of "the state
    directories Builder uses", which is exactly the kind of list this
    command's own docstring says to avoid. `job.stdout`/`job.stderr` are the
    one exception: `remove_jobs` already plans and removes those directly, as
    the job's own log files, so deriving a top-level name from them too would
    have this plan and that removal both name — and `--dry-run` both print —
    the same path.

    **EVERY RESOLVED STRING IS CHECKED AGAINST THE TEMPLATE IT CAME FROM
    BEFORE IT CAN NAME ANYTHING**, never trusted on its own. `jobs` and
    `templates` are the same declarations, filtered and ordered identically —
    one resolved, one not — by `resolved_jobs` and `raw_jobs` sharing their
    `load_jobs`/`as_invoker` filtering, so `placeholders._all_strings` yields
    both in lockstep. A resolved field counts as a candidate only when its
    *template* uses no placeholder beyond `{state}` (or, for the
    `.orient-control` check, `{home}`): of the few other placeholders a
    declaration can use (`{root}`, `{deploy}`, `{bin}`, `{python}`), `{root}`
    is the one read from `~/.config/convivy/session-agent.env`'s
    `CREWS_ROOT` (`configured_root()` below), which any agent session can
    write — and `jobs/library.toml` substitutes it straight into
    `LIBRARY_KNOWLEDGE_ROOT = "{root}/knowledge"` and into a `prepare` step's
    own argv, either of which makes an attacker-chosen `CREWS_ROOT` of
    `{state}/<any-name>` resolve to a string whose first segment below
    `state` is `<any-name>`, chosen entirely by whoever wrote that config
    file. Gating on the template rather than banning one field (`environment`,
    say) is what keeps this working for a field that legitimately derives a
    name from `{state}` alone, such as `ORIENT_CONFIG_ROOT = "{state}/orient"`,
    while refusing one that only looks the same after an agent-writable value
    has been substituted into it.
    """
    orient_control = f"{home}/{ORIENT_CONTROL_NAME}"
    state_norm = os.path.normpath(state)
    names: set[str] = set()
    has_orient_control = False
    for job, template in zip(jobs, templates):
        job_log_paths = {p for p in (job.stdout, job.stderr) if p}
        resolved_strings = list(placeholders._all_strings(job))
        template_strings = list(placeholders._all_strings(template))
        for text, raw in zip(resolved_strings, template_strings):
            if text in job_log_paths:
                continue
            used = set(placeholders.PLACEHOLDER.findall(raw))
            if text == orient_control or text.startswith(orient_control + "/"):
                if used <= {"home"}:
                    has_orient_control = True
                continue
            if not used <= {"state"}:
                continue
            top = _state_top_name(state_norm, text)
            if top:
                names.add(top)
    return names, has_orient_control


def data_paths_present(state: str) -> list[Path]:
    """The Library's store and the backup directory, whichever exist."""
    state_path = Path(state)
    return [state_path / name for name in (LIBRARY_NAME, BACKUP_NAME)
            if (state_path / name).exists()]


def decide_data_removal(state: str, dry_run: bool, keep_data: bool, confirm) -> bool | None:
    """Whether the Library's store and the backups should be removed, decided
    BEFORE anything else is touched — never as the last of several steps.

    **ASKED FIRST, SO A REFUSAL TO ASK LEAVES EVERYTHING ELSE UNTOUCHED
    TOO.** Deciding this only when `apply_state_plan` got around to it would
    mean a non-interactive run had already stopped every job, removed every
    deploy area and every MCP registration before discovering it could not
    answer the one question it needed to — a half-finished uninstall that
    contradicts the "nothing was changed" a refusal is supposed to mean.

    Returns `None` when there is nothing to decide (neither path exists, or
    this is a dry run, which only reports the question rather than asking it).
    """
    paths = data_paths_present(state)
    if not paths:
        return None
    if dry_run:
        listed = ", ".join(str(p) for p in paths)
        print(f"  would ask whether to remove the Library's store and your backups: {listed}")
        print("    (skip the question with --keep-data)")
        return None
    if keep_data:
        return False
    listed = ", ".join(str(p) for p in paths)
    return confirm(
        f"Remove the Library's store and your backups? This holds your work queue, "
        f"your knowledge repository's local copy of what the Library has indexed, and "
        f"every backup Builder has taken ({listed})."
    )


def apply_state_plan(names: set[str], has_orient_control: bool, home: str, state: str,
                      dry_run: bool, remove_data: bool | None, keep_data: bool,
                      left: list[tuple[str, str]], removed: list[str]) -> None:
    state_path = Path(state)
    # `install-jobs`'s own record is never added here: `remove_jobs` already
    # removes it unconditionally, jobs present or not, and both functions
    # naming the same path is exactly the `--dry-run` double "would remove"
    # this fix exists to stop.
    auto = sorted(names - {LIBRARY_NAME, BACKUP_NAME})
    for name in dict.fromkeys(auto):
        path = state_path / name
        if dry_run:
            if os.path.lexists(path):
                print(f"  would remove: {path}")
            continue
        if remove_path(path, left):
            removed.append(str(path))
    if has_orient_control:
        path = Path(home) / ORIENT_CONTROL_NAME
        if dry_run:
            if os.path.lexists(path):
                print(f"  would remove: {path}")
        elif remove_path(path, left):
            removed.append(str(path))

    if dry_run or remove_data is None:
        return
    data_paths = data_paths_present(state)
    reason = ("holds the adopter's work; kept because --keep-data was given" if keep_data
             else "holds the adopter's work; kept on the operator's answer")
    for path in data_paths:
        if remove_data:
            if remove_path(path, left):
                removed.append(str(path))
        else:
            left.append((str(path), reason))


def default_confirm(prompt: str) -> bool:
    if not sys.stdin.isatty():
        raise Refused(
            f"{prompt}\nThis needs an answer and stdin is not a terminal. Pass "
            f"--keep-data to keep the Library's store and your backups without "
            f"being asked, or run this where you can answer. Nothing was changed."
        )
    answer = input(f"{prompt} [y/N] ").strip().lower()
    return answer in ("y", "yes")


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The knowledge index: agent-platform's own ~/.knowledge-index, which no job
# declaration in jobs/ names.
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------

KnowledgeIndex = namedtuple("KnowledgeIndex", "dirname filenames")

# agent-platform's bin/knowledge-autopull, bin/agentctl and bin/co all
# hardcode Path.home()/.knowledge-index for the derived index and its own
# logs, so this is read by content rather than off a job declaration the way
# the rest of this file reads what it removes.
KNOWLEDGE_INDEX = KnowledgeIndex(
    dirname=".knowledge-index",
    filenames=frozenset({"index.db", "index.db-shm", "index.db-wal", "autopull.log", "watcher.log"}),
)


def apply_knowledge_index_plan(home: str, dry_run: bool, left: list[tuple[str, str]],
                                removed: list[str]) -> None:
    """Remove `{home}/.knowledge-index` only when every entry in it is one
    of `KNOWLEDGE_INDEX.filenames` — the derived index and its own logs,
    never the adopter's own work. A directory holding anything else, or
    being a symlink, is left and named, the same way an unstamped deploy
    area is."""
    path = Path(home) / KNOWLEDGE_INDEX.dirname
    if not os.path.lexists(path):
        return
    if path.is_symlink():
        left.append((str(path), "it is a symlink; refusing to remove through it"))
        return
    try:
        entries = {entry.name for entry in path.iterdir()}
    except OSError as exc:
        left.append((str(path), f"could not be read ({exc.strerror or exc})"))
        return
    extra = entries - KNOWLEDGE_INDEX.filenames
    if extra:
        left.append((str(path), f"holds {', '.join(sorted(extra))}, which this install does "
                                f"not recognize as part of the derived index, so it is left"))
        return
    if dry_run:
        print(f"  would remove: {path}")
        return
    # EACH KNOWN FILE, THEN THE EMPTY DIRECTORY, AND NEVER `rmtree`. The check
    # above reads names only, so an entry carrying a known name could be a
    # directory; unlinking refuses one rather than descending into it. And
    # agentctl and co can write here while this runs, so anything that appears
    # after the check leaves the directory non-empty, and `rmdir` leaves it.
    for name in sorted(entries):
        try:
            os.unlink(path / name)
        except OSError as exc:
            left.append((str(path / name), f"could not be removed ({exc.strerror or exc})"))
    try:
        os.rmdir(path)
    except OSError as exc:
        left.append((str(path), f"could not be removed ({exc.strerror or exc})"))
        return
    removed.append(str(path))


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The machine-wide instruction file bin/install-payload placed from this
# release's own deploy area, compared and removed before that area goes.
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------

# bin/install-payload's own MACHINE_SOURCE: the machine-wide instruction
# file it copies, never links, from the builder-payload deploy area's own
# knowledge-seed/machine/CLAUDE.md to {home}/.claude/CLAUDE.md.
MACHINE_CLAUDE_MD_SOURCE_REL = os.path.join("knowledge-seed", "machine", "CLAUDE.md")


def apply_machine_claude_md_plan(payload_area: Path, home: Path, dry_run: bool,
                                  left: list[tuple[str, str]], removed: list[str]) -> None:
    """Remove `{home}/.claude/CLAUDE.md` only when it is byte-identical to
    the machine-wide instruction file this release's own `builder-payload`
    deploy area would place (`MACHINE_CLAUDE_MD_SOURCE_REL` under
    `payload_area`).

    **READ WHILE `payload_area` STILL EXISTS.** The caller runs this before
    `apply_deploy_plan` removes that area, since the shipped copy to compare
    against lives nowhere else. Every session on the machine reads this file
    at start, so a file that differs — an edit, or a copy from a different
    release — or a missing shipped copy to compare against, is left and
    named rather than guessed at; an adopter's own edit is never removed.
    """
    target = home / ".claude" / "CLAUDE.md"
    if not os.path.lexists(target):
        return
    source = payload_area / MACHINE_CLAUDE_MD_SOURCE_REL
    if not source.is_file():
        left.append((str(target), "the machine-wide instruction file this install shipped, to "
                                   "compare it against, is missing; left in place"))
        return
    if target.is_symlink():
        left.append((str(target), "it is a symlink; refusing to remove through it"))
        return
    try:
        live = target.read_bytes()
    except OSError as exc:
        left.append((str(target), f"could not be read ({exc.strerror or exc})"))
        return
    if live != source.read_bytes():
        left.append((str(target), "its content differs from the machine-wide instruction file "
                                   "this install shipped, so it may hold an edit; left in place"))
        return
    if dry_run:
        print(f"  would remove: {target}")
        return
    if remove_path(target, left):
        removed.append(str(target))


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Deploy areas: install-backend's REPOSITORIES and COMPONENTS, plus
# install-payload's own payload repository.
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------


def deploy_targets(deploy: Path, home: Path) -> list[tuple[str, Path, Path]]:
    """`(name, path to remove, stamp file that must exist first)`, one per
    area `install-backend` or `install-payload` places."""
    targets = []
    for name in ib.REPOSITORIES:
        area = deploy / name
        targets.append((name, area, area / ib.STAMP))
    for component in ib.COMPONENTS:
        lib = component.lib_at(home)
        targets.append((component.name, lib, lib / ib.STAMP))
    payload_area = deploy / PAYLOAD_REPOSITORY
    targets.append((PAYLOAD_REPOSITORY, payload_area, payload_area / PAYLOAD_STAMP))
    return targets


def component_venvs(home: Path) -> dict[str, Path]:
    """Each component's venv, home-relative, removed alongside its `lib`."""
    return {component.name: component.venv_at(home) for component in ib.COMPONENTS}


def apply_deploy_plan(deploy: Path, home: Path, dry_run: bool,
                       left: list[tuple[str, str]], removed: list[str]) -> None:
    """Remove every stamped deploy area.

    **CALLED AFTER `apply_payload_skills`, NEVER BEFORE.** This removes the
    `builder-payload` area whole, `skills/` subdirectory included; a caller
    that unlinks the skills `install-payload` placed by looking inside that
    same `skills/` directory has to do so while it still exists, or it finds
    nothing to check and leaves a dangling symlink behind instead of a clean
    unlink.
    """
    venvs = component_venvs(home)
    for name, area, stamp in deploy_targets(deploy, home):
        if not os.path.lexists(area):
            continue
        if not stamp.is_file():
            left.append((str(area), "exists but carries no install stamp, so it was "
                                    "not placed by this install"))
            continue
        if dry_run:
            print(f"  would remove deploy area: {name} ({area})")
            venv = venvs.get(name)
            if venv is not None and os.path.lexists(venv):
                print(f"  would remove: {venv}")
            continue
        if remove_path(area, left):
            removed.append(str(area))
        venv = venvs.get(name)
        if venv is not None and remove_path(venv, left):
            removed.append(str(venv))


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Linked commands, skills (both link-commands' own and install-payload's),
# and the session hook.
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------


def apply_link_plan(deploy: Path, bin_dir: Path, skills_dir: Path, settings: Path,
                     home: Path, dry_run: bool, left: list[tuple[str, str]],
                     removed: list[str]) -> None:
    items = lc.plan(deploy, bin_dir, skills_dir, commands=True, settings=settings, home=home)
    for item in items:
        if item["name"] == lc.HOOK_NAME:
            apply_hook_removal(item, settings, dry_run, left, removed)
            continue
        if item["name"] == lc.PROFILE_NAME:
            apply_profile_removal(item, dry_run, left, removed)
            continue
        link = item["link"]
        if item["state"] == "ours":
            if dry_run:
                print(f"  would unlink: {link}")
            elif unlink_path(link, left):
                removed.append(str(link))
        elif item["state"] == "occupied":
            left.append((str(link), "holds something this install's own link-commands run "
                                    "did not put there"))


def apply_hook_removal(item: dict, settings: Path, dry_run: bool,
                        left: list[tuple[str, str]], removed: list[str]) -> None:
    if item["state"] != "ours":
        return
    if dry_run:
        print(f"  would remove the SessionStart hook from: {settings}")
        return
    try:
        changed = remove_hook(Path(os.path.realpath(settings)), item["command"])
    except lc.Refused as exc:
        left.append((str(settings), str(exc)))
        return
    if changed:
        removed.append(f"{settings} (SessionStart hook)")
    else:
        left.append((str(settings), "the hook was not found when this ran, even though "
                                    "link-commands reported it as linked"))


def remove_hook(path: Path, command: str) -> bool:
    """Remove the one `hooks.SessionStart` entry whose command is `command`,
    leaving the rest of `path` exactly as it is. Mirrors `link-commands`'s
    own `place_hook`: same read, same atomic replace, same symlink-following
    target, run the other direction."""
    settings = lc.read_settings(path)
    groups = lc.session_start_groups(settings, path)
    new_groups = []
    changed = False
    for group in groups:
        entries = group.get("hooks", []) if isinstance(group, dict) else []
        if not isinstance(entries, list):
            new_groups.append(group)
            continue
        kept = [e for e in entries
                if not (isinstance(e, dict) and e.get("command") == command)]
        if len(kept) != len(entries):
            changed = True
        if kept:
            new_groups.append({**group, "hooks": kept})
        # A group this leaves with no hooks is dropped rather than kept
        # empty: it was this install's own `{"matcher": ..., "hooks": [...]}`
        # entry, and an empty one left behind is litter nothing else wrote.
    if not changed:
        return False
    settings.setdefault("hooks", {})[lc.HOOK_EVENT] = new_groups
    try:
        mode = path.stat().st_mode & 0o777
    except FileNotFoundError:
        mode = 0o600
    fd, staging = tempfile.mkstemp(prefix=f".{path.name}.uninstall-builder.", dir=path.parent)
    try:
        with os.fdopen(fd, "w") as out:
            json.dump(settings, out, indent=2)
            out.write("\n")
            os.fchmod(out.fileno(), mode)
        os.replace(staging, path)
    except BaseException:
        try:
            os.unlink(staging)
        except OSError:
            pass
        raise
    return True


def apply_profile_removal(item: dict, dry_run: bool, left: list[tuple[str, str]],
                           removed: list[str]) -> None:
    if item["state"] != "ours":
        return
    link = item["link"]
    if dry_run:
        print(f"  would remove the PATH line from: {link}")
        return
    try:
        changed = remove_profile_line(Path(os.path.realpath(link)), item["line"])
    except lc.Refused as exc:
        left.append((str(link), str(exc)))
        return
    if changed:
        removed.append(f"{link} (PATH line)")
    else:
        left.append((str(link), "the PATH line was not found when this ran, even though "
                                "link-commands reported it as present"))


def remove_profile_line(path: Path, line: str) -> bool:
    """Remove the one line `line` from `path`, exactly, leaving every other
    byte — and the file itself, even once this leaves it empty — exactly as
    it is. Mirrors `link-commands`'s own `place_profile_line`: same read,
    same atomic replace, same symlink-following target, run the other
    direction. A line the operator wrote themselves, without the exact
    marker text this compares against, is never a match and stays."""
    try:
        text = path.read_text()
    except FileNotFoundError:
        return False
    except OSError as exc:
        raise lc.Refused(f"could not read {path} ({exc.strerror or exc})") from None
    lines = text.splitlines(keepends=True)
    kept = [one for one in lines if one.rstrip("\n") != line]
    if len(kept) == len(lines):
        return False
    try:
        mode = path.stat().st_mode & 0o777
    except OSError:
        mode = 0o644
    fd, staging = tempfile.mkstemp(prefix=f".{path.name}.uninstall-builder.", dir=path.parent)
    try:
        with os.fdopen(fd, "w") as out:
            out.write("".join(kept))
            os.fchmod(out.fileno(), mode)
        os.replace(staging, path)
    except BaseException:
        try:
            os.unlink(staging)
        except OSError:
            pass
        raise
    return True


def apply_payload_skills(payload_area: Path | None, skills_dir: Path, dry_run: bool,
                          left: list[tuple[str, str]], removed: list[str]) -> None:
    """The skills `install-payload` links directly — never through
    `link-commands` — found the same way `install-payload` itself checks a
    live path: against what is in the deploy area's own `skills/`."""
    if payload_area is None:
        return
    skills_source = payload_area / "skills"
    if not skills_source.is_dir():
        return
    for src in sorted(skills_source.iterdir()):
        if not src.is_dir():
            continue
        name = src.name
        live = skills_dir / name
        if not os.path.lexists(live):
            continue
        if live.is_symlink() and Path(os.readlink(live)) == skills_source / name:
            if dry_run:
                print(f"  would unlink: {live}")
            elif unlink_path(live, left):
                removed.append(str(live))
        else:
            left.append((str(live), "holds something install-payload's own link did not put there"))


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# MCP registrations.
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------


def apply_mcp_plan(deploy: Path, dry_run: bool, left: list[tuple[str, str]],
                    removed: list[str]) -> None:
    items = rm.plan(deploy)
    present = [item for item in items if item["state"] == "present"]
    for item in items:
        if item["state"] == "mismatch":
            left.append((item["name"], "registered with something this install did not put there"))
    if not present:
        return
    if dry_run:
        for item in present:
            print(f"  would remove MCP registration: {item['name']}")
        return
    cli = rm.claude_cli()
    for item in present:
        done = subprocess.run([cli, "mcp", "remove", item["name"], "-s", "user"],
                              capture_output=True, text=True, check=False, timeout=120)
        still = rm.registered_servers().get(item["name"])
        if still is not None:
            left.append((item["name"], f"`claude mcp remove` did not clear it "
                                       f"(exit {done.returncode})"))
        else:
            removed.append(f"MCP registration: {item['name']}")


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Main.
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------


def configured_root(home: str) -> str:
    """The Builder root this machine is configured with, read the way
    `bin/install-payload`'s own `configured_root()` reads it: the first
    `CREWS_ROOT=` line in the session agent's config, or the documented
    default when there is none."""
    config = Path(home) / ".config" / "convivy" / "session-agent.env"
    try:
        text = config.read_text(encoding="utf-8")
    except OSError:
        text = ""
    for line in text.splitlines():
        stripped = line.strip()
        if not stripped.startswith("CREWS_ROOT"):
            continue
        _, _, value = stripped.partition("=")
        value = value.strip()
        if value[:1] in ("'", '"') and value[-1:] == value[:1] and len(value) >= 2:
            value = value[1:-1]
        if value == "~":
            return home
        if value.startswith("~/"):
            return f"{home}/{value[2:]}"
        if value:
            return value
    return f"{home}/builder"


def main_single_account(args: argparse.Namespace) -> int:
    platform = args.platform or detect_platform()
    home = args.home or str(Path.home())
    # `register-mcp`'s own `config_path()` reads `$HOME` from the
    # environment rather than from any argument, since it has to agree
    # with whatever `claude` itself resolves. Setting it here is what
    # makes `--home` (a test's throwaway one, or the real account's) the
    # one this process, and anything it execs, actually acts on.
    os.environ["HOME"] = home
    deploy = args.deploy or f"{home}/.local/deploy"
    bin_dir = args.bin_dir or f"{home}/.local/bin"
    skills_dir = args.skills or f"{home}/.claude/skills"
    settings = args.settings or f"{home}/.claude/settings.json"
    state = args.state or f"{home}/.local/state"
    jobs_dir = args.jobs or (REPO / "jobs")
    root = configured_root(home)

    print(f"{PROG}: {platform}, {home}")

    left: list[tuple[str, str]] = []
    removed: list[str] = []

    # DECIDED BEFORE ANYTHING ELSE IS TOUCHED. Asking this only once every
    # other step had already run would mean a non-interactive refusal left
    # jobs stopped, deploy areas gone and MCP registrations removed before
    # it discovered it could not answer the one question it needed to —
    # the opposite of what a refusal is supposed to mean here.
    remove_data = decide_data_removal(state, args.dry_run, args.keep_data, default_confirm)

    if declarations_present(jobs_dir):
        jobs = resolved_jobs(jobs_dir, platform, home, root, deploy, state, bin_dir)
        templates = raw_jobs(jobs_dir, platform)
    else:
        jobs = []
        templates = []
        print(f"  no job declarations at {jobs_dir}; nothing to stop or remove for jobs")

    remove_jobs(jobs, platform, home, state, args.dry_run, left, removed)

    apply_link_plan(Path(deploy), Path(bin_dir), Path(skills_dir), Path(settings),
                    Path(home), args.dry_run, left, removed)

    # The payload skills are unlinked, and the machine-wide instruction file
    # compared, while the builder-payload deploy area, which they point into
    # or must be read from, still exists — removing that area first would
    # leave a dangling symlink behind instead of a clean unlink (see
    # apply_deploy_plan's own docstring), and leave the machine-wide file
    # with nothing to compare against.
    payload_area = Path(deploy) / PAYLOAD_REPOSITORY
    apply_payload_skills(payload_area, Path(skills_dir), args.dry_run, left, removed)
    apply_machine_claude_md_plan(payload_area, Path(home), args.dry_run, left, removed)
    apply_deploy_plan(Path(deploy), Path(home), args.dry_run, left, removed)

    apply_mcp_plan(Path(deploy), args.dry_run, left, removed)

    names, has_orient_control = state_plan(jobs, templates, home, state)
    apply_state_plan(names, has_orient_control, home, state, args.dry_run, remove_data,
                     args.keep_data, left, removed)

    apply_knowledge_index_plan(home, args.dry_run, left, removed)

    if args.dry_run:
        print(f"\n{PROG}: dry run; nothing was changed")
    else:
        print(f"\n{PROG}: removed {len(removed)} item(s)")
    if left:
        print(f"{PROG}: left {len(left)} item(s) in place:")
        for path, reason in left:
            print(f"  {path}: {reason}")
    else:
        print(f"{PROG}: nothing was left behind that this install is aware of")
    print(
        f"\n{PROG}: never touched: the Builder root (crews, the knowledge repository, "
        f"the steward's charter), the operator config under ~/.config/convivy, and "
        f"any secret you supplied yourself, such as the LLM gateway's credentials file. "
        f"Those are yours, not this install's."
    )
    return 0


def main_two_account(args: argparse.Namespace, prefix: str,
                      system: pa.System | None = None, drop=None) -> int:
    """Remove the two-account shape: root's own half, over `prefix` and the
    agents account's own system-scope jobs, by the three-account shape's own
    code for every piece the two shapes share; everything in the agents
    home, run by a child that has dropped to the `agents` account first;
    then the account.

    **NEVER PROBES FOR A `reviewers` ACCOUNT, A SUDOERS FILE OR A REVIEW
    LAUNCHER.** `install-privileged --shape two` places none of the three,
    so this never looks for them: there is no `sudoers_dir` here to read,
    and `pa.remove` is called with shape "two", which touches only `agents`.

    **`drop` IS `run_as_agents_account`'s OWN, THREADED THROUGH FOR A TEST
    THAT CANNOT BECOME `agents`.** A real run never passes it, and gets
    `bin/install-jobs`'s own `drop_to_account`.
    """
    if os.geteuid() != 0:
        raise Refused(
            f"removing the two-account shape stops the `{AGENTS}` account's own "
            f"system-scope jobs, removes the root-owned tree under {prefix}, and "
            f"removes the `{AGENTS}` account itself — all of which need root. Run "
            f"this with sudo. Nothing was removed."
        )

    platform = args.platform or detect_platform()
    agents_home = args.agents_home or su.agents_home()
    deploy = args.deploy or f"{agents_home}/.local/deploy"
    bin_dir = args.bin_dir or f"{agents_home}/.local/bin"
    skills_dir = args.skills or f"{agents_home}/.claude/skills"
    settings = args.settings or f"{agents_home}/.claude/settings.json"
    state = args.state or f"{agents_home}/.local/state"
    jobs_dir = args.jobs or (REPO / "jobs")
    log_dir = args.log_dir or f"{prefix}/var/log"
    root = configured_root(agents_home)

    # The same `System` `pa.remove` uses at the foot of this function, asked
    # early for the `agents` account's own uid and gids too, the same reason
    # `main_three_account` asks early: `run_as_agents_account` needs them to
    # drop to that account before anything below touches a path inside
    # `agents_home`.
    account_system = system or pa.system_for_platform()
    account = account_system.account(AGENTS)
    if account is None:
        raise Refused(
            f"the `{AGENTS}` account no longer exists on this machine. "
            f"Nothing was removed."
        )
    ids = AccountIds(pw_name=account.name, pw_uid=account.uid, pw_gid=account.gid)

    print(f"{PROG}: two-account, {platform}, {AGENTS} home {agents_home}, prefix {prefix}")

    left: list[tuple[str, str]] = []
    removed: list[str] = []

    # DECIDED BEFORE ANYTHING ELSE IS TOUCHED, for the same reason the
    # single-account and three-account shapes decide it first.
    remove_data = decide_data_removal(state, args.dry_run, args.keep_data, default_confirm)

    if declarations_present(jobs_dir):
        jobs = resolved_agents_jobs(jobs_dir, platform, agents_home, root, log_dir,
                                     deploy, state, bin_dir)
        templates = agents_system_jobs(jobs_dir, platform)
    else:
        jobs = []
        templates = []
        print(f"  no job declarations at {jobs_dir}; nothing to stop or remove for jobs")

    # ROOT'S OWN: the same system-scope jobs the three-account shape stops,
    # rendered and removed through the same function.
    remove_system_jobs(jobs, platform, args.system_dir, log_dir, agents_home, state,
                        args.dry_run, left, removed)

    def agents_home_steps() -> tuple[list[tuple[str, str]], list[str]]:
        """Everything the single-account shape removes, reused verbatim and
        pointed at the agents home, the same way `main_three_account`'s own
        `agents_home_steps` does.

        **`HOME` IS SET HERE, NOT BY THE CALLER.** See
        `main_three_account.agents_home_steps` for why.
        """
        os.environ["HOME"] = agents_home
        inner_left: list[tuple[str, str]] = []
        inner_removed: list[str] = []
        apply_link_plan(Path(deploy), Path(bin_dir), Path(skills_dir), Path(settings),
                        Path(agents_home), args.dry_run, inner_left, inner_removed)
        payload_area = Path(deploy) / PAYLOAD_REPOSITORY
        apply_payload_skills(payload_area, Path(skills_dir), args.dry_run, inner_left, inner_removed)
        apply_deploy_plan(Path(deploy), Path(agents_home), args.dry_run, inner_left, inner_removed)
        apply_mcp_plan(Path(deploy), args.dry_run, inner_left, inner_removed)
        names, has_orient_control = state_plan(jobs, templates, agents_home, state)
        apply_state_plan(names, has_orient_control, agents_home, state, args.dry_run, remove_data,
                         args.keep_data, inner_left, inner_removed)
        apply_knowledge_index_plan(agents_home, args.dry_run, inner_left, inner_removed)
        return inner_left, inner_removed

    agent_left, agent_removed = run_as_agents_account(ids, agents_home_steps, drop=drop)
    left.extend(agent_left)
    removed.extend(agent_removed)

    remove_privileged_tree(prefix, args.dry_run, left, removed)

    if args.dry_run:
        print(f"  would remove the `{AGENTS}` account through provision-accounts "
              f"--shape two (pass --purge-homes to also delete a home that still "
              f"holds something)")
        print(f"\n{PROG}: dry run; nothing was changed")
    else:
        try:
            removed.extend(pa.remove(account_system, "two", args.purge_homes))
        except pa.Refused as exc:
            left.append((f"the `{AGENTS}` account", str(exc)))
        print(f"\n{PROG}: removed {len(removed)} item(s)")
    if left:
        print(f"{PROG}: left {len(left)} item(s) in place:")
        for path, reason in left:
            print(f"  {path}: {reason}")
    else:
        print(f"{PROG}: nothing was left behind that this install is aware of")
    print(
        f"\n{PROG}: never touched: GitHub's own state (the App installation, and the "
        f"branch rules on main) — removing this machine's install never reaches "
        f"GitHub, so that stays until you remove it there yourself; and any secret "
        f"you supplied by hand. Those are yours, not this install's."
    )
    return 0


def main_three_account(args: argparse.Namespace, prefix: str,
                        system: pa.System | None = None, drop=None) -> int:
    """Remove the three-account shape: root's own half, over `prefix` and
    the agents account's own system-scope jobs; everything the single-account
    shape removes, reused pointed at the agents home, run by a child that
    has dropped to the `agents` account first; then the accounts.

    **`drop` IS `run_as_agents_account`'s OWN, THREADED THROUGH FOR A TEST
    THAT CANNOT BECOME `agents`.** A real run never passes it, and gets
    `bin/install-jobs`'s own `drop_to_account`.
    """
    if os.geteuid() != 0:
        raise Refused(
            f"removing the three-account shape stops the `{AGENTS}` account's own "
            f"system-scope jobs, removes the root-owned tree under {prefix} and its "
            f"one sudo rule, and removes the `{AGENTS}` and `{REVIEWERS}` accounts "
            f"themselves — all of which need root. Run this with sudo. Nothing was "
            f"removed."
        )

    platform = args.platform or detect_platform()
    agents_home = args.agents_home or su.agents_home()
    deploy = args.deploy or f"{agents_home}/.local/deploy"
    bin_dir = args.bin_dir or f"{agents_home}/.local/bin"
    skills_dir = args.skills or f"{agents_home}/.claude/skills"
    settings = args.settings or f"{agents_home}/.claude/settings.json"
    state = args.state or f"{agents_home}/.local/state"
    jobs_dir = args.jobs or (REPO / "jobs")
    sudoers_dir = args.sudoers_dir or DEFAULT_SUDOERS_DIR
    log_dir = args.log_dir or f"{prefix}/var/log"
    root = configured_root(agents_home)

    # The same `System` `pa.remove` uses at the foot of this function, asked
    # early for the `agents` account's own uid and gids too: `run_as_agents_
    # account` needs them to drop to that account before anything below
    # touches a path inside `agents_home`, and a `FakeAccountSystem` answers
    # this the same way it already answers the shape detection above.
    account_system = system or pa.system_for_platform()
    account = account_system.account(AGENTS)
    if account is None:
        raise Refused(
            f"the `{AGENTS}` account no longer exists on this machine. "
            f"Nothing was removed."
        )
    ids = AccountIds(pw_name=account.name, pw_uid=account.uid, pw_gid=account.gid)

    print(f"{PROG}: three-account, {platform}, {AGENTS} home {agents_home}, prefix {prefix}")

    left: list[tuple[str, str]] = []
    removed: list[str] = []

    # DECIDED BEFORE ANYTHING ELSE IS TOUCHED, for the same reason the
    # single-account shape decides it first (see `main_single_account`).
    # This asks only whether the Library's store and the backups should go,
    # never touching a path itself, so it stays root's, the way the system
    # jobs below stay root's.
    remove_data = decide_data_removal(state, args.dry_run, args.keep_data, default_confirm)

    if declarations_present(jobs_dir):
        jobs = resolved_agents_jobs(jobs_dir, platform, agents_home, root, log_dir,
                                     deploy, state, bin_dir)
        templates = agents_system_jobs(jobs_dir, platform)
    else:
        jobs = []
        templates = []
        print(f"  no job declarations at {jobs_dir}; nothing to stop or remove for jobs")

    # ROOT'S OWN: the agents account's system-scope jobs are root's own,
    # machine-wide units naming `agents` through `UserName=`/`User=`, never
    # that account's own per-user job — see `stop_system_job`.
    remove_system_jobs(jobs, platform, args.system_dir, log_dir, agents_home, state,
                        args.dry_run, left, removed)

    def agents_home_steps() -> tuple[list[tuple[str, str]], list[str]]:
        """Everything the single-account shape removes, reused verbatim and
        pointed at the agents home: the three-account shape's `place`,
        `link` and `register` steps are `agents`' own, the same installers
        the single-account shape runs for the operator. Run here, inside
        the child `run_as_agents_account` has already dropped to `agents`,
        so a path inside its home is only ever read or written by that
        account, and `claude mcp remove` (through `apply_mcp_plan`) execs
        with that account's own `HOME`, never root's.

        **`HOME` IS SET HERE, NOT BY THE CALLER.** Setting it before the
        fork would leave it on root's own environment for the rest of this
        run; set here, it only ever exists in this one dropped child.
        """
        os.environ["HOME"] = agents_home
        inner_left: list[tuple[str, str]] = []
        inner_removed: list[str] = []
        apply_link_plan(Path(deploy), Path(bin_dir), Path(skills_dir), Path(settings),
                        Path(agents_home), args.dry_run, inner_left, inner_removed)
        payload_area = Path(deploy) / PAYLOAD_REPOSITORY
        apply_payload_skills(payload_area, Path(skills_dir), args.dry_run, inner_left, inner_removed)
        apply_deploy_plan(Path(deploy), Path(agents_home), args.dry_run, inner_left, inner_removed)
        apply_mcp_plan(Path(deploy), args.dry_run, inner_left, inner_removed)
        names, has_orient_control = state_plan(jobs, templates, agents_home, state)
        apply_state_plan(names, has_orient_control, agents_home, state, args.dry_run, remove_data,
                         args.keep_data, inner_left, inner_removed)
        apply_knowledge_index_plan(agents_home, args.dry_run, inner_left, inner_removed)
        return inner_left, inner_removed

    agent_left, agent_removed = run_as_agents_account(ids, agents_home_steps, drop=drop)
    left.extend(agent_left)
    removed.extend(agent_removed)

    remove_sudo_rule(prefix, sudoers_dir, args.dry_run, left, removed)
    remove_privileged_tree(prefix, args.dry_run, left, removed)

    if args.dry_run:
        print(f"  would remove the `{AGENTS}` and `{REVIEWERS}` accounts through "
              f"provision-accounts --shape three (pass --purge-homes to also delete "
              f"a home that still holds something)")
        print(f"\n{PROG}: dry run; nothing was changed")
    else:
        try:
            removed.extend(pa.remove(account_system, "three", args.purge_homes))
        except pa.Refused as exc:
            left.append((f"the `{AGENTS}` and `{REVIEWERS}` accounts", str(exc)))
        print(f"\n{PROG}: removed {len(removed)} item(s)")
    if left:
        print(f"{PROG}: left {len(left)} item(s) in place:")
        for path, reason in left:
            print(f"  {path}: {reason}")
    else:
        print(f"{PROG}: nothing was left behind that this install is aware of")
    print(
        f"\n{PROG}: never touched: GitHub's own state (the App installation, and the "
        f"branch rules on main) — removing this machine's install never reaches "
        f"GitHub, so that stays until you remove it there yourself; and any secret "
        f"you supplied by hand. Those are yours, not this install's."
    )
    return 0


def main(argv: list[str], system: pa.System | None = None, drop=None) -> int:
    parser = argparse.ArgumentParser(
        prog=PROG,
        description="Remove a Builder install.",
    )
    parser.add_argument("--home", help="the operator's home directory, for the "
                        "single-account shape")
    parser.add_argument("--agents-home", help="the agents account's home directory, "
                        "for the two-account and three-account shapes (default: the "
                        "directory service's)")
    parser.add_argument("--deploy", help="deploy root (default: {home}/.local/deploy)")
    parser.add_argument("--bin", dest="bin_dir", help="linked commands directory "
                        "(default: {home}/.local/bin)")
    parser.add_argument("--skills", help="skills directory (default: {home}/.claude/skills)")
    parser.add_argument("--settings", help="Claude Code settings file "
                        "(default: {home}/.claude/settings.json)")
    parser.add_argument("--state", help="writable state directory (default: {home}/.local/state)")
    parser.add_argument("--jobs", type=Path, help="directory of .toml job declarations "
                        "(default: jobs/ beside this checkout)")
    parser.add_argument("--platform", choices=("macos", "linux"),
                        help="act as this platform instead of the one we are on")
    parser.add_argument("--prefix", help="the two-account or three-account shape's "
                        "privileged install root (default: /opt/builder)")
    parser.add_argument("--sudoers-dir", help="where the one sudo rule lives, for the "
                        "three-account shape alone (default: /etc/sudoers.d)")
    parser.add_argument("--log-dir", help="the root-owned directory the agents "
                        "account's system-scope jobs log to (default: {prefix}/var/log)")
    parser.add_argument("--system-dir", help="where a system-scope job's unit lives, "
                        "instead of /Library/LaunchDaemons or /etc/systemd/system")
    parser.add_argument("--purge-homes", action="store_true",
                        help="with the two-account or three-account shape, also delete "
                        "a home that still holds something once provision-accounts "
                        "--remove would otherwise leave it")
    parser.add_argument("--dry-run", action="store_true",
                        help="say what would be removed, change nothing")
    parser.add_argument("--keep-data", action="store_true",
                        help="keep the Library's store and your backups, without being asked")
    args = parser.parse_args(argv)

    try:
        prefix = args.prefix or DEFAULT_PREFIX
        # An injected `system` answers the shape's own account question too,
        # so a test's FakeAccountSystem is the one source of truth for both
        # "does `agents` exist" and "removes the accounts" — never the real
        # directory service once a test has supplied its own.
        exists = (lambda name: system.account(name) is not None) if system is not None else None
        shape = refuse_unsupported_shape(exists=exists, prefix=prefix)
        if shape == "three":
            return main_three_account(args, prefix, system=system, drop=drop)
        if shape == "two":
            return main_two_account(args, prefix, system=system, drop=drop)
        return main_single_account(args)

    except (Refused, ij.Refused, lc.Refused, rm.Refused, ib.Refused, pa.Refused) as exc:
        print(f"{PROG}: refusing — {exc}", file=sys.stderr)
        return 2


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main(sys.argv[1:]))
